Abstract

We present a rigorously specified, falsifiable theory of human–AI symbiosis synthesising the 2026 dynamical‑systems, information‑geometry, and verifiable‑reinforcement learning literatures. A four‑dimensional coupled ordinary differential equation governs the co‑evolution of human skill, data diversity, model capability, and delegation, with auxiliary dynamics incorporating knowledge‑collapse elasticity and performance‑driven adaptation. Through nullcline analysis and Jacobian characterisation we prove the existence of a transcritical bifurcation at a calibratable critical AI capability threshold K^* \approx 0.85 and derive the global basin boundary between autonomous and degenerate attractors. Innovation is formalised as quality‑constrained entropy maximisation on a Fisher–Rao manifold with guaranteed policy‑gradient convergence. The symbiotic synthesis loop operates under verifiable human reward, and diversity preservation is enforced by a barrier‑certified protocol that maintains a provable lower bound on data variety. Governance is embedded as hybrid switching control with forward‑invariant safe sets and a recovery guarantee whenever unaided practice exceeds a 20% floor. The theoretical apparatus is supplemented by explicit falsifiability criteria tied to longitudinal trajectory observables and pre‑registered bifurcation tests. The theory makes no empirical claims; it provides a logically complete, mathematically testable foundation for building and scrutinizing symbiotic human–AI systems.

