1. Introduction

The substitution‑versus‑symbiosis debate is no longer a matter of philosophical preference but of dynamical inevitability. Empirically, naive human–AI collaboration degrades human skill, homogenises output, and triggers model autophagy (Shumailov et al., 2024; Ju & Aral, 2026; Park et al., 2026). Theoretically, recent work by Wu et al. (2026), Huang et al. (2026), and Acemoglu et al. (2026) shows that such outcomes are not accidental but arise from generic features of coupled learning–delegation feedback loops.

The present paper unifies these insights into a single, closed‑form theoretical framework. Its principal technical contributions are:

1. A unified four‑variable ODE system (Section 2) that integrates the minimal feedback model of Wu et al., the performance‑driven delegation dynamics of Huang et al., and the knowledge‑collapse elasticity of Acemoglu et al., with an explicit vector field, auxiliary relations, and a full equilibrium and bifurcation analysis. The transcritical bifurcation at the critical AI capability threshold K^* is derived analytically from nullcline intersections, and the basin boundary is characterised by a three‑dimensional stable manifold of a hyperbolic interior saddle.
2. A quality‑constrained entropy maximisation framework (Section 3) grounded in the Fisher–Rao metric on a latent semantic manifold, with convergence guarantees for the entropy‑regularised policy gradient and a geodesic innovation distance that is contraction‑mapped under verifiable constraints.
3. A synthesis architecture with verifiable human feedback (Section 4) that embeds the diversity preservation protocol as a dynamic forcing term in the ODE, with a barrier‑certified lower bound on data diversity.
4. Governance as hybrid feedback control (Section 5) where the offloading fraction p is modulated by a switching law with formal forward‑invariance and recovery proofs, and the epistemological debt ceiling is bounded via a Lyapunov argument.
5. Operational falsifiability (Section 6) through pre‑registered longitudinal trajectories, bifurcation detection tests, and specified statistical criteria for rejecting the symbiotic, deskilling, diversity, and governance claims.

The paper is non‑empirical; its value lies in providing a logically coherent and mathematically explicit target for refutation.

