2. The Unified Coupled Dynamical System

2.1 Model Specification

We consider a representative human–AI ecosystem where a population of human professionals interacts with a single continuously updated AI model. The state at time t \ge 0 is

\mathbf{x}(t) = \big(H(t), D(t), M(t), p(t)\big)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^4,

with each variable normalised to [0,1]:

· H(t) – mean human skill in the target task,
· D(t) – diversity of human‑originated data entering the model’s training stream,
· M(t) – AI model capability (expected task success rate),
· p(t) – fraction of tasks delegated to the AI by the human.

The dynamics are governed by

\begin{aligned}
\dot{H} &= \alpha_H (H+\varepsilon)(1-H)\,(1-p) - \beta_H H p + r_H I_{\text{unaided}}(t), \\[4pt]
\dot{D} &= \gamma_D H\,(1 - \eta A) - \delta_D A + r_D - \theta_D (D - D_{\text{ext}}), \\[4pt]
\dot{M} &= \gamma_M D - \delta_M S + r_M, \\[4pt]
\dot{p} &= \kappa\, p(1-p) \big[\ell(H) - \ell(M)\big],
\end{aligned}

with auxiliary relations

A = \alpha_A p M,\qquad S = \beta_S A,\qquad \ell(z) = (1-z)^2.

The new term -\theta_D (D - D_{\text{ext}}) incorporates knowledge‑collapse elasticity (Acemoglu et al., 2026): the diversity carried forward in public knowledge decays toward a common‑pool value D_{\text{ext}} at rate \theta_D when the human‑originated stream is insufficiently novel. Parameters are summarised in Table 1; all are strictly positive except r_H, r_D, r_M which may be zero in uncontrolled conditions.

