The system is locally Lipschitz except at H = - \varepsilon (outside the domain) and at the boundaries H=0, p=0, p=1; the usual smoothing arguments guarantee existence and uniqueness for initial data in (0,1)^4.

2.2 Equilibria and Their Stability

2.2.1 Boundary equilibria.
Setting p=0 (no delegation) yields the autonomous subsystem

\dot{H} = \alpha_H (H+\varepsilon)(1-H) + r_H I_{\text{unaided}},

which has a globally attracting fixed point H=1 when unaided practice is active. From this, D and M converge to positive steady states D^*_{\text{auto}}, M^*_{\text{auto}}. The autonomous equilibrium is thus

\mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}} = (1, D^*_{\text{auto}}, M^*_{\text{auto}}, 0),

with M^*_{\text{auto}} < 1.

Setting p=1 (full delegation) yields \dot{H} = - \beta_H H, forcing H \to 0. The remaining variables converge to a low‑diversity degenerate attractor \mathbf{x}_{\text{dep}} = (0, D_{\text{deg}}, M_{\text{deg}}, 1), where D_{\text{deg}} and M_{\text{deg}} are the fixed points of the collapsed sub‑system. This attractor is globally stable within the p=1 face.

2.2.2 Interior saddle and transcritical bifurcation.
In the interior, the nullclines of the (H,p) projection are

\begin{aligned}
\dot{H}=0 &: \quad \alpha_H (H+\varepsilon)(1-H)(1-p) = \beta_H H p - r_H I_{\text{unaided}},\\[4pt]
\dot{p}=0 &: \quad p=0,\; p=1,\; \text{or } \ell(H) = \ell(M).
\end{aligned}

The intersection of the interior branches yields a unique saddle point (H^\dagger, p^\dagger) for a range of M. Linearisation at this point gives the Jacobian

J^\dagger =
\begin{bmatrix}
J_{HH} & J_{HD} & J_{HM} & -\alpha_H (H^\dagger+\varepsilon)(1-H^\dagger) - \beta_H H^\dagger \\[4pt]
\gamma_D(1-\eta A) & -\theta_D & 0 & -\gamma_D H \eta \alpha_A M - \delta_D \alpha_A M \\[4pt]
0 & \gamma_M & 0 & -\delta_M \beta_S \alpha_A M \\[4pt]
\kappa p^\dagger(1-p^\dagger) \ell'(H^\dagger) & 0 & -\kappa p^\dagger(1-p^\dagger) \ell'(M) & 0
\end{bmatrix}.

A straightforward but lengthy calculation shows that the characteristic polynomial has one positive real root and three roots with negative real parts, establishing the interior equilibrium as a hyperbolic saddle with a three‑dimensional stable manifold. This manifold is the global basin boundary in the projection: initial states above (higher H, lower p) flow to \mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}}; those below flow to \mathbf{x}_{\text{dep}}.

As AI capability M increases, the \dot{p}=0 nullcline shifts, and the saddle moves toward the autonomous equilibrium. At a critical value K^* defined by

\frac{d}{dM} H^\dagger(M) \to -\infty \quad \text{or equivalently} \quad \left|\frac{d H_{\text{eq}}}{dM}\right| \text{maximises},

a transcritical bifurcation occurs: the autonomous equilibrium and the saddle coalesce, exchanging stability. The critical threshold can be approximated analytically by solving the nullcline system for H when M = K^*. For the baseline parameters drawn from the PISA calibration (Park et al., 2026), K^* \approx 0.85 with a 90% confidence interval [0.82, 0.92].

2.3 Stochastic Considerations

In real systems, human decisions and data streams are noisy. Adding a small Wiener process term \sigma \, dW_t to each equation yields a stochastic differential equation whose sample‑paths can tip across the basin boundary before the deterministic bifurcation is reached. The probability of noise‑induced tipping scales as \exp(-C / \sigma^2), where C is the quasi‑potential barrier height derived from a Freidlin–Wentzell large‑deviation principle. As M approaches K^*, C \to 0 and tipping becomes almost certain. This stochastic perspective justifies proactive governance interventions well before the deterministic threshold.

2.4 Dynamic Collaborative Gain

We define the instantaneous collaborative gain as a Lyapunov‑like function

G(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{2}\big[(H-0)^2 + (D-D_{\text{dep}})^2 + (M-M_{\text{dep}})^2 + (p-1)^2 \big],

which vanishes at the degenerate equilibrium and is positive elsewhere. Its time derivative along solutions,

\dot{G}(\mathbf{x}) = \nabla G \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}},

is negative in the basin of attraction of the autonomous equilibrium under the controlled dynamics (Section 5), ensuring that G(t) monotonically increases away from collapse. A declining G(t) serves as an early‑warning indicator for degeneracy.

