4. Synthesis Architecture with Verifiable Human Feedback

4.1 Intent as Formal Constraint Set

The human architect translates a strategic objective into a triple \langle \mathcal{C}, Q_{\min}, \mathcal{D} \rangle, where \mathcal{D} is a domain‑constraint operator selecting an admissible sub‑manifold \mathcal{Y}_C \subset \mathcal{Y}. The verifiable reward is

R_{\text{verify}}(x,y) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}} \mathbf{1}[y \text{ satisfies } c].

4.2 Entropy‑Regularised RLVF

The AI’s policy is updated via

\theta \leftarrow \theta + \eta \nabla_\theta \big[ R_{\text{verify}}(x,y) + \lambda(t)\, H(\pi_\theta(\cdot|x)) \big],

with \lambda(t) adapted by the diversity controller. Using the C3PO protocol (Stephan et al., 2024), human qualitative feedback is converted into additional verifiable constraints on a small synthetic dataset, after which the policy is fine‑tuned with a divergence penalty to prevent unlearning on unrelated tasks.

4.3 Diversity Preservation as Barrier‑Certified Control

The human‑relative crowding ratio \rho (Azad et al., 2026) is an online observable:

\rho(t) = \frac{\text{mean pairwise geodesic distance of model‑only outputs}}{\text{mean pairwise geodesic distance of unaided human outputs}}.

We couple \rho to the ODE through the diversity decay term \delta_D. In particular, whenever \rho(t) < 1, the effective drift toward D_{\text{dep}} accelerates. To counteract this, the diversity protocol activates three measures:

1. Diverse persona prompting, increasing the output set’s covering radius.
2. A step increase in \lambda(t) to raise policy entropy.
3. Cross‑domain injection at every k‑th iteration, modelled as a periodic forcing term F_{\text{cross}}(t) added to \dot{D}:

\dot{D} = \cdots + \gamma_{\text{cross}} \, \mathbf{1}_{\text{cross}}(t) \, (1-D).

Lemma 1 (Diversity lower bound). Assume the cross‑domain injection has duty cycle \tau_{\text{on}} / T and amplitude \gamma_{\text{cross}}. Then there exists a barrier function B(D) = -\ln(D-D_{\min}) such that, under the combined actions of diverse prompting and cross‑domain injection, \dot{B} \le 0 on the set \{D > D_{\min}\}, guaranteeing that D(t) \ge D_{\min} > 0 for all t if initially above. The value D_{\min} can be made arbitrarily close to D_{\text{ext}} by increasing \gamma_{\text{cross}}.

Thus the architecture provably prevents collapse to zero diversity.

4.4 Symbiotic Innovation Index (SII)

The SII is a weighted sum

\text{SII} = w_H \Delta H_{\text{QEMPO}} + w_G d_{\text{FR}}(y_{\text{final}},\mu_{\text{AI}}) + w_S (1 - \text{SMM}_{\text{discrepancy}}),

where the weights w_i are domain‑specific and can be set by multi‑objective calibration on a validation set of example interactions via a Pareto‑front analysis. All components are normalised to [0,1].

