5. Governance as Hybrid Switching Control

5.1 Control Law

The Symbiotic Governance Authority (SGA) adjusts the allowed delegation fraction p_{\text{allowed}}(t) according to a hybrid automaton with three states:

· Normal: p_{\text{allowed}} = p_{\text{nom}} as long as H \ge H_{\text{safe}} and \rho \ge 1.
· Conservative: If H < H_{\text{safe}} or \rho < 1, reduce p_{\text{allowed}} to p_{\text{red}} \in (p_{\text{floor}}, p_{\text{nom}}).
· Critical: If H < H_{\text{crit}}, enforce p_{\text{allowed}} = p_{\text{floor}} \equiv 0.2.

Transitions are smoothed by a dwell‑time condition to prevent chattering.

5.2 Forward Invariance and Recovery

Theorem 1 (Safe set invariance). Consider the closed‑loop system with the hybrid control law. Let the safe set be \mathcal{S} = \{ \mathbf{x} : H \ge H_{\text{safe}}, p \le p_{\text{nom}} \}. If initially H(0) \ge H_{\text{safe}} and the parameters satisfy \alpha_H (H_{\text{safe}}+\varepsilon)(1-H_{\text{safe}})(1-p_{\text{nom}}) > \beta_H H_{\text{safe}} p_{\text{nom}}, then \mathcal{S} is forward invariant under the deterministic dynamics. Moreover, when the system enters the Critical state, the subsystem

\dot{H} = \alpha_H (H+\varepsilon)(1-H)(1-p_{\text{floor}}) - \beta_H H p_{\text{floor}} + r_H,

possesses a unique stable equilibrium H^* > 0, guaranteeing recovery of skill above the critical threshold within finite time.

Proof sketch. Forward invariance follows from the barrier certificate B(\mathbf{x}) = H_{\text{safe}} - H; its derivative along the flow is negative at the boundary of \mathcal{S} by the condition on \dot{H}. The existence and stability of H^* are obtained from the sign change of the right‑hand side. \square

5.3 Epistemological Debt Bound

Epistemological debt E(t) models the accumulated gap between task complexity (driven by AI assistance) and human comprehension. It evolves as

\dot{E} = \alpha_E \, A_{\text{assist}} - \beta_E \, (1-p) \, E,

with A_{\text{assist}} = \alpha_A p M. Under the Critical state p = p_{\text{floor}}, the term -\beta_E (1-p_{\text{floor}})E provides exponential decay, bounding E by E_{\max} = \frac{\alpha_E \alpha_A p_{\text{floor}}}{\beta_E (1-p_{\text{floor}})}. This provably prevents the runaway accumulation of incomprehension.

