6. Falsifiability through Pre‑Registered Experimentation

Each core theoretical claim is paired with an operationalisable refutation condition.

1. Symbiotic synergy. Conduct a within‑subjects pre‑registered trial for task class \tau. After 100 closed‑loop interactions, test the one‑sided hypothesis \text{SII}_{\text{symbiotic}} > \text{SII}_{\text{AI‑only}}. If not rejected with power 1-\beta \ge 0.8 at \alpha=0.05, the synergy claim is unsupported.
2. Critical deskilling threshold. In a longitudinal study with n participants, manipulate AI capability M across a fine grid around K^* and measure equilibrium skill H_{\text{eq}}. Fit a piecewise regression; if the transition at K^* is not significantly steeper than a linear model (Chow test, \alpha=0.01), the transcritical bifurcation model is rejected.
3. Diversity collapse. Monitor \rho(t) for 50 consecutive interaction sessions under baseline delegation. If \rho never drops below parity, the diversity‑collapse risk is falsified for that domain. If the cross‑domain injection protocol fails to restore \rho \ge 1 within five sessions after a drop, the diversity‑preservation claim is rejected.
4. Governance failure. Deploy the SGA‑controlled system in a field setting. If any participant’s H falls below H_{\text{crit}} for two successive monthly audit cycles despite the Critical protocol being active, the governance architecture is deemed insufficient.

These criteria transform the theoretical architecture into a target for rigorous empirical scrutiny.

