Appendix A – Mathematical Background and Notation

A.1 The Coupled ODE System

We study the autonomous system

\begin{cases}
\dot{H} = \alpha_H (H+\varepsilon)(1-H)(1-p) - \beta_H H p + r_H I_u(t),\\
\dot{D} = \gamma_D H (1-\eta A) - \delta_D A + r_D - \theta_D (D-D_{\text{ext}}),\\
\dot{M} = \gamma_M D - \delta_M S + r_M,\\
\dot{p} = \kappa\, p(1-p) \big[ \ell(H) - \ell(M) \big],
\end{cases}

with auxiliary definitions

A = \alpha_A p M,\qquad S = \beta_S A,\qquad \ell(z)=(1-z)^2,

and where I_u(t) is a piecewise constant indicator of scheduled unaided practice (value 0 or 1). All state variables are confined to [0,1]. The parameters are strictly positive unless otherwise noted.

A.2 Fisher–Rao Geometry

Let a generative model define a family of probability distributions over outputs y with parameter \theta. The Fisher information matrix F(\theta) induces a Riemannian metric on the parameter space. For a latent semantic manifold \mathcal{Y} embedded via a continuous mapping \phi:\Theta \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}, the pullback metric is g_{ij}(\theta) = F_{ij}(\theta). The geodesic distance between two points \theta_1,\theta_2 (and hence between outputs) is

d_{\text{FR}}(\theta_1,\theta_2) = \inf_{\gamma} \int_0^1 \sqrt{ \dot{\gamma}(t)^\top F(\gamma(t)) \dot{\gamma}(t) }\,dt.

A.3 Quality‑Constrained Entropy Maximisation (QEMPO)

Given a policy \pi_\theta mapping context x to a distribution over \mathcal{Y}, the Shannon (or differential) entropy is

H(\pi_\theta(\cdot|x)) = -\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_\theta} \log \pi_\theta(y|x).

Define the expected verifiable quality Q(x,y) \in [0,1] derived from constraints \mathcal{C}. The QEMPO problem is

\max_\theta \; \mathbb{E}_x\left[ H(\pi_\theta(\cdot|x)) \right] \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_\theta}[Q(x,y)] \ge Q_{\min}.

The Lagrangian is \mathcal{L}(\theta,\lambda) = \mathbb{E}[H(\pi_\theta)] + \lambda (\mathbb{E}[Q] - Q_{\min}), with \lambda\ge 0 adapted during training.

A.4 Verifiable Reward (RLVF)

The verifiable reward is the fraction of constraints satisfied:

R_{\text{verify}}(x,y) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|} \sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}} \mathbf{1}[y \text{ satisfies } c].

A.5 Human‑Relative Crowding Ratio \rho

Let \mu_{\text{AI}} be the set of n AI‑only generated outputs for a given task, and \mu_{\text{human}} the set of m unaided human outputs. Then

\rho = \frac{ \frac{1}{n(n-1)} \sum_{i\ne j} d(\mu_{\text{AI}}^{(i)}, \mu_{\text{AI}}^{(j)}) }{ \frac{1}{m(m-1)} \sum_{i\ne j} d(\mu_{\text{human}}^{(i)}, \mu_{\text{human}}^{(j)}) },

where d is the Fisher–Rao geodesic distance. A value \rho < 1 indicates AI‑induced diversity collapse.

A.6 Control Law and Governance

The governance automaton modulates p_{\text{allowed}}(t) according to:

p_{\text{allowed}} =
\begin{cases}
p_{\text{nom}} & \text{if } H \ge H_{\text{safe}} \text{ and } \rho \ge 1,\\
p_{\text{red}} & \text{if } H < H_{\text{safe}} \text{ or } \rho < 1,\\
p_{\text{floor}} & \text{if } H < H_{\text{crit}},
\end{cases}

with hysteresis and minimum dwell time T_{\text{dwell}} to avoid chattering.

