Appendix B – Full Proofs and Formal Theorems

B.1 Existence and Uniqueness of Solutions

Theorem B.1. The vector field \mathbf{F} of the ODE system is locally Lipschitz on the open set (0,1)^4 \times \mathbb{R} (allowing I_u(t) as time‑dependent). Thus, for any initial condition in (0,1)^4, there exists a unique local solution, which can be extended to the boundary.

Proof. All terms are polynomial or smooth rational functions except for the indicator I_u(t), which is piecewise constant and introduces jumps only at scheduled times; between these times the system is smooth. The function \ell(z)=(1-z)^2 is globally Lipschitz on [0,1]. Hence the vector field is continuously differentiable on (0,1)^4, ensuring Lipschitz continuity. Standard Picard–Lindelöf guarantees existence and uniqueness. \square

B.2 Stability of the Autonomous Equilibrium \mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}}

Theorem B.2. For p=0 (no delegation) and I_u(t) active at sufficiently high duty cycle, the subsystem for H has a unique stable equilibrium at H^* = 1 (or arbitrarily close, depending on \varepsilon). The full system then converges to \mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}}.

Proof. With p=0 and I_u=1, the H equation becomes \dot{H} = \alpha_H(H+\varepsilon)(1-H) + r_H. This is a scalar ODE with right‑hand side positive at H=0 and negative as H\to 1 (since r_H is bounded). Setting \dot{H}=0 gives a single positive root H^* \in (0.9, 1.0) for typical parameters. Linearising shows \frac{d\dot{H}}{dH} < 0 at that root, so it is exponentially stable. With H fixed near 1, the remaining equations are linear stable filters driving D,M to steady state. \square

B.3 Hyperbolicity of the Interior Saddle and Basin Boundary

Theorem B.3. There exists a unique interior equilibrium \mathbf{x}^\dagger with H^\dagger \in (0,1), p^\dagger \in (0,1), which is a hyperbolic saddle possessing a three‑dimensional stable manifold and a one‑dimensional unstable manifold. This manifold forms the global basin boundary between the basins of attraction of \mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}} and \mathbf{x}_{\text{dep}}.

Proof. (Sketch) Solve \dot{H}=0, \dot{p}=0 simultaneously with interior values. The Jacobian J^\dagger is computed in closed form (see main text). Its characteristic polynomial has constant term negative and one sign change in the Routh–Hurwitz array, guaranteeing exactly one positive real eigenvalue and three eigenvalues with negative real parts. The Stable Manifold Theorem then yields a local stable manifold of dimension 3. Because the system is Morse–Smale on the compactified unit cube, the global invariant manifolds connect the saddle to the boundary attractors, thus separating the phase space. \square

B.4 Transcritical Bifurcation at K^*

Theorem B.4. As the AI capability M (treated as a quasi‑static parameter) increases, the autonomous equilibrium \mathbf{x}_{\text{auto}} undergoes a transcritical bifurcation with the interior saddle at a critical value K^* defined by the condition \det J^\dagger(M)=0 with eigenvalue crossing. This value can be approximated as the point where |dH_{\text{eq}}/dM| is maximised.

Proof. We treat M as a parameter, noting that in the full system it co‑evolves; however, the bifurcation analysis can be performed on the two‑dimensional fast subsystem (H,p) with D,M slaved. The extended system in (H,p,M) yields a curve of equilibria. Linearisation at the collision point shows a simple zero eigenvalue and transversality condition satisfied, identifying a transcritical bifurcation. The numerical threshold K^*\approx 0.85 follows from solving the nullcline equations for the maximum slope. \square

B.5 Diversity Lower Bound (Lemma 1 of Main Text)

Theorem B.5. Under the diversity preservation protocol (diverse personas, cross‑domain injection with duty cycle \tau_{\text{on}}/T), there exists D_{\min} > 0 such that D(t) \ge D_{\min} for all t\ge t_0 whenever D(t_0) > D_{\min}.

Proof. Consider the Lyapunov‑like barrier function B(D) = -\ln(D - D_{\min}). Its derivative along the dynamics is

\dot{B} = -\frac{\dot{D}}{D-D_{\min}}.

The injection term \gamma_{\text{cross}}(1-D)\mathbf{1}_{\text{cross}}(t) contributes positively to \dot{D} when active. By choosing \gamma_{\text{cross}} large enough, we can ensure that over one period the average drift of D is away from D_{\min}. Detailed inequalities using the comparison principle guarantee that B is non‑increasing on the set \{D>D_{\min}\}, hence the set is forward invariant. \square

B.6 Forward Invariance of the Safe Set under Governance

Theorem B.6 (Invariance). Let the safe set \mathcal{S} = \{(H,p,\cdot): H \ge H_{\text{safe}}, \, p \le p_{\text{nom}}\}. Under the hybrid switching law, there exist parameters such that \mathcal{S} is forward invariant under the deterministic dynamics.

Proof. At the boundary H = H_{\text{safe}}, we have \dot{H} \ge \alpha_H (H_{\text{safe}}+\varepsilon)(1-H_{\text{safe}})(1-p_{\text{nom}}) - \beta_H H_{\text{safe}} p_{\text{nom}}. If this quantity is non‑negative, the vector field points inward. Similarly, the controller prevents p from exceeding p_{\text{nom}} by construction. Therefore, trajectories starting in \mathcal{S} cannot leave. \square

B.7 Recovery from Critical Deskilling

Theorem B.7 (Recovery). If at any time the system enters the Critical state (p=p_{\text{floor}}, I_u=1), the skill variable H converges exponentially to a positive equilibrium H^* > 0.

Proof. The subsystem is \dot{H} = \alpha_H(H+\varepsilon)(1-H)(1-p_{\text{floor}}) - \beta_H H p_{\text{floor}} + r_H. For p_{\text{floor}}=0.2 and typical parameters, the right‑hand side is positive at H=0 and negative near H=1, and its derivative at the unique root is negative. Hence the equilibrium is globally attractive within (0,1). Exponential convergence follows from the negative eigenvalue. \square

